          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does antibiotic stewardship guidance say about upper respiratory infections?",
          "answer": "Antibiotic stewardship guidance emphasizes that most acute upper respiratory infections, including the common cold, many sore throats, acute bronchitis, and uncomplicated acute sinusitis in its early stages, are caused by viruses. For these conditions, antibiotics do not shorten the illness or prevent complications effectively enough to warrant routine use.\n\nUsing antibiotics in these situations can cause harm through adverse effects, allergic reactions, disruption of normal gut flora, *Clostridioides difficile* infection, and promoting the development of antibiotic-resistant organisms. Therefore, not prescribing antibiotics for these presentations is an active recommendation.\n\nGuidance highlights the importance of communicating with patients. This includes explaining the expected course and duration of the illness, describing when the plan might need to change, and suggesting ways to manage symptoms.\n\nThis information is from the \"Infectious diseases society and public health antimicrobial stewardship guidance (educational summary)\" document titled \"Upper respiratory infection: antibiotic stewardship and when antibiotics are indicated.\"\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
